.TH "PERFORMANCERESOURCETIMING.TRANSFERSIZE" "3JS" "October 27, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
PerformanceResourceTiming.transferSize \- PerformanceResourceTiming:
transferSize property
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]transferSize\f[B]\f[R] read\-only property represents the
size (in octets) of the fetched resource.
This is the size of the response body plus a fixed value that stands in
for the size of the response headers.
.SH VALUE
The \f[CR]transferSize\f[R] property can have the following values:
.IP \(bu 2
A number representing the size (in octets) of the fetched resource.
This is the size of the encoded response body (see
\f[CR]encodedBodySize\f[R]) plus 300.
The fixed value of 300 stands in for the size of the response headers,
because their real size could reveal information such as the presence of
cookies.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]300\f[R] if the resource was revalidated with the server, rather
than downloaded again.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]0\f[R] if the resource was retrieved from a local cache without
contacting the server.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]0\f[R] if the resource is a cross\-origin request and doesn\(cqt
pass the \f[CR]Timing\-Allow\-Origin\f[R] check.
.SH DESCRIPTION
For a cross\-origin resource, \f[CR]transferSize\f[R] is reported only
if the resource is served with a \f[CR]Timing\-Allow\-Origin\f[R] header
that allows the requesting origin.
Without this header, \f[CR]transferSize\f[R] is \f[CR]0\f[R], whether or
not the resource was retrieved from a cache.
.PP
For example, to allow \f[CR]https://developer.mozilla.org\f[R] to see
transfer sizes, the cross\-origin resource should send:
.IP
.EX
Timing\-Allow\-Origin: https://developer.mozilla.org
.EE
.PP
The part of \f[CR]transferSize\f[R] that represents the size of the
response body is subject to the same CORS restriction as
\f[CR]encodedBodySize\f[R].
If a cross\-origin resource passes the \f[CR]Timing\-Allow\-Origin\f[R]
check but not the CORS check, \f[CR]transferSize\f[R] doesn\(cqt include
the body size.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Checking if a cache was hit
For environments not supporting the \f[CR]responseStatus\f[R] property,
the \f[CR]transferSize\f[R] property can be used to determine cache
hits.
If \f[CR]transferSize\f[R] is zero and the resource has a non\-zero
decoded body size, the resource was fetched from a local cache.
This check works only for same\-origin resources and for cross\-origin
resources that pass both the \f[CR]Timing\-Allow\-Origin\f[R] check and
the CORS check.
Without \f[CR]Timing\-Allow\-Origin\f[R], \f[CR]transferSize\f[R] is
always \f[CR]0\f[R].
.PP
Example using a \f[CR]PerformanceObserver\f[R], which notifies of new
\f[CR]resource\f[R] performance entries as they are recorded in the
browser\(cqs performance timeline.
Use the \f[CR]buffered\f[R] option to access entries from before the
observer creation.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] observer = \f[B]new\f[R] PerformanceObserver((list) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  list.getEntries().forEach((entry) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    \f[B]if\f[R] (entry.transferSize === 0 && entry.decodedBodySize > 0) {
      console.log(\(ga${entry.name} was loaded from cache\(ga);
    }
  });
});

observer.observe({ type: \(dqresource\(dq, buffered: \f[B]true\f[R] });
.EE
.PP
Example using \f[CR]Performance.getEntriesByType()\f[R], which only
shows \f[CR]resource\f[R] performance entries present in the
browser\(cqs performance timeline at the time you call this method:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] resources = performance.getEntriesByType(\(dqresource\(dq);
resources.forEach((entry) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  \f[B]if\f[R] (entry.transferSize === 0 && entry.decodedBodySize > 0) {
    console.log(\(ga${entry.name} was loaded from cache\(ga);
  }
});
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Timing\-Allow\-Origin\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Access\-Control\-Allow\-Origin\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
